This product builds on the following work.

Kokoro-82M
  Text-to-speech model by hexgrad, Apache License 2.0.
  https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M
  speak downloads the ONNX export of the model and its voices from the kokoro-onnx project at
  first run; the weights are not part of this repository or of speak's binaries.

kokoro-onnx (https://github.com/thewh1teagle/kokoro-onnx), MIT License, Copyright (c) thewh1teagle
  speak's Kokoro backend follows kokoro-onnx 0.4.7's inference pipeline (phoneme batching,
  style selection, silence trimming), and src/vocab.rs is generated from its config.json.

misaki (https://github.com/hexgrad/misaki), Apache License 2.0, by hexgrad
  The espeak-ng to misaki phoneme conversion tables in src/g2p/espeak.rs are taken from
  misaki 0.9.4 (misaki/espeak.py). src/g2p/neural.rs ports misaki's FallbackNetwork
  (misaki/en.py, commit fba1236595f2d2bf21d414ba6e57d25256afada3).

misaki-rs (https://github.com/MicheleYin/misaki-rs), MIT License
  English grapheme-to-phoneme conversion, including misaki's lexicons, linked into speak.

graphemes_to_phonemes_en_us and graphemes_to_phonemes_en_gb, by PeterReid, Apache License 2.0
  https://huggingface.co/PeterReid/graphemes_to_phonemes_en_us (commit a5631b285d18d59483c32c0c3379cb9fac924f4b)
  https://huggingface.co/PeterReid/graphemes_to_phonemes_en_gb (commit d8357d5067fa26a5c34134d6bbcf4bbf000c0ac8)
  The neural grapheme-to-phoneme models misaki uses for English words its lexicons don't know.
  speak redistributes them: converted to ONNX by scripts/export-g2p.py (weights unchanged; the
  graph is one decoding step), stored in assets/g2p/ and embedded in speak's binary. The Apache
  License 2.0 text is in LICENSE-APACHE.

ONNX Runtime (https://onnxruntime.ai), MIT License, Copyright (c) Microsoft
  Corporation. Version 1.31.0, linked statically into speak's binary (prebuilt by pyke.io for
  the `ort` crate). Its licence and third-party notices are in licenses/onnxruntime/.

librosa (https://github.com/librosa/librosa), ISC License
  src/trim.rs implements the silence trimming of librosa.effects.trim, as kokoro-onnx uses it.

espeak-ng (https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng), GNU GPL version 3 or later
  speak never links or loads espeak-ng. When available, it runs the separate `espeak-ng`
  program for non-English voices and for English words its lexicons don't know.
  scripts/build-espeak.sh builds espeak-ng 1.52.0 from its unmodified release source. Release
  archives include that build and its data in libexec/speak/, its licence in
  licenses/espeak-ng-COPYING, and each release offers the exact source next to the archives.

The Rust crates linked into speak, and their licence texts, are listed in
licenses/THIRD-PARTY.md in each release archive (generated by cargo-about from about.toml).
